!! T14, the verb math (2758-2832, call sites 2834-2905)
# Span: `computeMismatch` 2762-2796, `coupleSignal` 2801-2804, `crystallizeSignal` 2810-2822, `signalOrExtract` 2828-2832; consumers `daemonImpl.coupleR` 2851-2859, `.mismatch` 2866-2875, `.flow` 2876-2899. 2. computeMismatch defaults column names `s{i}`, runs `coupleMesh` over single-column signals, asks the py/R pipe for `coupleR`, then diffs directed edges (ts te>0 vs R edge set) into `{agree, edges[], disagreements, note}`; with R absent it returns `{agree:null, rAvailable:false, edges:[]}`. coupleSignal is `coupleMesh` over the matrix. crystallizeSignal folds the matrix into (stratum=column, ordinal=row, strength=value) occurrences and calls mesh `crystallize`. signalOrExtract returns explicit rows, else reads the target's child streams from disk. 3. State: computeMismatch reads `captureFor(root)` (closure Map of py holders, fs-keyed by root) and awaits a child process; the other three are pure given inputs; signalOrExtract reads fs via `extractSignalFromTarget` (signal.json sidecars). No clock, env or globals. 4. Callers: only `daemonImpl` and the `runFlow` deps object (2883-2890); runFlow is node/src/flow-run.ts (imports mesh FLOW_SEEDS; lares-cli has its own flow-run command that routes to the daemon verb). Callees: mesh `coupleMesh` (mesh-couple.ts), `crystallize` (crystallization.ts), node `extractSignalFromTarget`. 5. Tests: coupling math is tested in mesh (mesh-couple, mesh-coupling-mv, couple-oracle, crystallization, flow). The glue (default names, edge diff, the agree/note logic, the empty-R branch, auto-extract precedence "explicit signal always wins") has NO direct test: grep finds the names only in open-node-vessel.ts.
# CAN IT RUN IN A DAEMON-ONLY TW5 `*-vm` MODULE? Yes for the math, with three conditions I measured:
#* Purity: the whole import closure of `coupleMesh` and `crystallize` is relative-only leaf modules (mesh-couple -> arl-dial, cmi-significance, mesh-coupling-mv, mesh-coupling, signed-innovation; those -> gaussian-cmi, sensorium-pc, transfer-entropy; crystallization -> nucleation-gate, temporal-rigidity -> numerics). I read their import lines: none pull Automerge, node builtins, or the barrel. A grep for `Math.random|Date.now|performance|process.|require(` across those ten files returned nothing, so they are deterministic and clockless.
#* Subpath: <code>&#64;lararium/mesh</code> exports only `./mesh-coupling` (TE/types) and `./worldline`/`./harvest` as pure subpaths (package.json:51,63,131); `coupleMesh` lives in `mesh-couple.ts` and `crystallize` in `crystallization.ts`, with NO subpath export. The VM modules must import pure subpaths (worldline-read-vm.ts:68 comment: the barrel drags wasm the plugin build cannot bundle). So the lane needs two new subpath exports (`./mesh-couple`, `./crystallization`) in the same PR.
#* Build: plugin-build compiles any TS with a `/*\ title/type/module-type: startup \*/` header (tw5-header.ts) into a cjs module; the pattern is `export const name`, pure `computeX` functions, and `startup()` publishing `$tw.lares.<x>Vm` (worldline-read-vm.ts 135-163; query-derive-vm.ts 80-90). A new module also needs registering in the plugin json via discover-modules (lares-memetic-wikitext.json lists the two existing).
The wire: today these run on the host with no hop. In the VM, `daemonImpl.mismatch` must post a `daemon:*-request` to the island and await `daemon:*-result` as worldline-compare does (daemon-vm-core.ts 267-277, 585-592; daemon-behavior.ts:294). New failure modes: VM cold or island fault (worldline's handler catches and returns an `error` result), rows serialized over the channel, async where `coupleSignal` is sync today (the `runFlow` deps `crystallize`/`couple` are typed sync at flow-run.ts:26-29; they would turn async, rippling into runFlow). The mismatch split is `host: await coupleR(...)` -> ship `{rows, names, rEdges}` -> `VM: diffMismatch`.
NAME WHAT MUST STAY ON THE HOST BINDING: `captureFor(...).coupleR/phase/forecast/capture/sweep/analyze/refresh/status/worldlineDag/cascadeKapae/subagentEdges/planeRecord` (py serve-op pipe, fs flocks); `extractSignalFromTarget` and `signalOrExtract` (reads signal.json off disk; the host must read and ship `rows`); `readCoupling/readCohere/readJing` (fs sensorium planes); the sensorium lifecycle executors (`senseRoster/Inspect/Build/Reconcile/Promote/Retire/UnRetire/Purge`, manifest.json fs, and `guardHitl`, whose approval capability must be verified on the host before any irreversible verb: a VM must never hold that gate); `telemetryImpl.writeback`; `mempalaceImpl`/`formImpl` (py content/form palaces, recall holders, `makeContentPalace`); `sovereignRecallClient`; `runFlow` dispatch (it chooses hulls and threads outcomes; keep the dispatcher host with injected handles). Sensorium state stays in py.
TIMING/MIRROR: the module must be daemon-only (T15): its title must never enter the glass mirror's plugin set; T15's witness should land first or in the same PR.
My judgment: the math is a better first cut as a plain pure module (node or mesh) than as a VM module. A pure `diffMismatch(tsTe, names, rEdges)`, `crystallizeSignal`, `coupleSignal` tested directly gives the testability win with zero channel, zero async ripple and no mirror hazard. The VM lift then adds only the sovereign-worker move (compute in the worker) at the price of a channel hop for a rarely-called verb.
# Laws: sovereign-worker primacy (argues for VM), no key on a surface (none involved), capability not host (the math is host-free), web3-only OK, no global now OK.
# Risk: sync->async ripple into runFlow; empty-signal behavior ("never fabricating a matrix", 2824-2827) must survive: an empty `rows` must still reach the verb's honest no-signal answer, not a VM error; R-absent branch must still return `agree:null` (never `true`). Collision: none in flight (the span has no u1/ralias hunk here).
# Red-first: (i) `diffMismatch` table: R absent -> `agree:null, edges:[]`; both agree; one-sided edge -> `agree:false` and counts in `disagreements`; names length mismatch -> `s{i}` defaults; control: a fixture matching mesh couple-oracle. (ii) crystallize: lone column never crystallizes (the comment says so). (iii) for the VM step: a startup-module test like worldline-read-vm.test.ts running the same table through `$tw.lares.<x>Vm`, plus a test that the title is absent from the mirror plugin set.
# Verdict: NEEDS-RULING. Fork: pure-module first then VM, or VM directly. Lean: pure module in this lane (CUT-READY by itself, includes the two mesh subpath exports only if the VM step follows), VM step as a second PR after T15's witness stands. The road row T14 as written ("verb math into a daemon-only *-vm") is feasible but skips the cheaper step.

