!! T17, deadlines injected (3386-3387; related 2579-2607, daemon-vm-core.ts:134-141, hearth-gate-pins.ts:132)
# node: open-node-vessel.ts:3386 `mountSilenceMs: 120_000`, 3387 `mountStallMs: 360_000`. 2. They set the pool's ea-breath silence window and progress-stall budget for each island mount (vessel-island-pool-core.ts:726,737-738 `awaitIslandMsg`; each `breath` re-arms the silence window, so a live long mount never times out; the stall budget bounds breathing without advancing). 3. Reads/writes no state; constants. Used in two timers per mount. 4. Only node site; the pool option is declared at vessel-island-pool.ts:77-79 and core :295-297,364-365,395-396 (default 10s / 3x). Tests set it: pool-mount-intensity.test.ts:42 (200ms), browser and mesh pool-plugin-resolution tests. 5. Tests reach the pool option directly, but no test reaches the vessel's chosen value (it sits behind a whole boot). 6. Lift target: a named node-side deadline source following the existing precedent `dialDeadlines()` (hearth-gate-pins.ts:132, "The node owns a clock, so dialDeadlines is here and not in mesh"): `mountDeadlines(opts?)` returning `{silenceMs, stallMs}`, defaulted at the node to 120_000/360_000 and overridable through `NodeVesselOptions` so a boot test can inject 200ms. Better still, one exported pair that both the node pool and `openDaemonVm`'s `eaSilenceMs/eaStallMs` read, so the three homes (F2) collapse to one. 7. Laws: no global now: these are durations that a causal breath re-arms ("silence alone times out", daemon-vm-core.ts:128-133), not wall-clock deadlines; the row is an injection and dedupe, not a clock removal. Say so in the brief so no lane deletes the stall budget. 8. Risk: F2 (an `undefined` falls to the pool's 10s and re-creates the false-timeout fatal boot). The daemon island's own silence budget (daemon-vm-core) must move in the same PR or the two diverge. Collisions: none. 9. Red-first: boot-level test injecting 200ms asserts a silent island refuses within the injected window (red today: the constant ignores it); control: no injection -> the pool receives 120_000/360_000 (assert the exact numbers at the constructor, since a default-fall-through to 10_000 is the failure), and the daemon island receives the same pair.
# Verdict: CUT-READY.

